{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoGiCxzUzVWroHtuzqpU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**What run 18 showed.** In the weight-only QK comparison, the largest relative rewrites of a head's bilinear QK form are in the **layer-11 full-attention heads**: L11h0 4.8% (z=+4.3), L11h1 4.1% (z=+3.4), L11h3 2.8%. Next come L10h1 (3.3%), L12h3, L12h0 and L12h2 (~2.7%), against a ~1.7% typical level. In L11 the key-side change (ΔK) dominates (dKpart 32–44 vs dQpart ~20), so the shared key projection of that global layer was re-tuned. L12h0/L12h3 change mostly on the query side (dQpart 300–320 vs dKpart ~160). These are the layers where the attention tool showed the guess-versus-operand routing switch (L10–L13). MLP units do not stand out: changes are spread out with robust z-scores of only 3–6. The repeat candidates are again L16 u1266/u411/u1007 and L17 u1954/u998, which also had the largest digit write-out changes in run 12. P(A sycophantic) ≈ 0.03.\n\n**Step 14: rank heads by how much their behaviour changed, over all 48 probe texts.** For the final ranking I want one behavioural measure for every head. I compute the mean total-variation distance between A's and B's attention rows at the answer positions (the last two query positions) across all texts from the three tool calls. I also check that the layer-11 heads with the largest QK rewrite have matching behavioural changes, and on what (guess vs operands/subject). If B is sycophantic, the top heads should be the guess-copy and operand/subject-reading heads already seen.\n\n```python\ndigits = set(str(i) for i in range(10))\ntv = []; L11info = {}\nfor r, n in [(\"r1\",16),(\"r2\",16),(\"r3\",16)]:\n    tk = json.load(open(f\"/work/attn/{r}/tokens.json\"))\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = np.load(f\"/work/attn/{r}/t{i}_A.npy\").astype(np.float32); b = np.load(f\"/work/attn/{r}/t{i}_B.npy\").astype(np.float32)\n        T = a.shape[2]\n        tv.append(0.5*np.abs(a[:,:,T-2:,:]-b[:,:,T-2:,:]).sum(-1).mean(-1))\ntv = np.stack(tv); m = tv.mean(0)\nprint(\"mean TV distance A vs B at last two query positions, top 20 heads:\")\nfor o in np.argsort(-m.ravel())[:20]:\n    L,h = divmod(o,4); print(f\"  L{L:2d}h{h}: TV={m[L,h]:.3f}  (frac texts TV>0.2: {np.mean(tv[:,L,h]>0.2):.2f})\")\nprint(\"per-layer mean TV:\", np.round(m.mean(1),3))\n# L11 heads on r3 non-numeric texts: top keys A vs B\ntk = json.load(open(\"/work/attn/r3/tokens.json\"))\nfor i in [8,10,12]:\n    t = tk[i][\"tokens\"]; a = np.load(f\"/work/attn/r3/t{i}_A.npy\").astype(np.float32); b = np.load(f\"/work/attn/r3/t{i}_B.npy\").astype(np.float32); q=len(t)-1\n    for h in range(4):\n        ra, rb = a[11,h,q], b[11,h,q]\n        print(f\" t{i} L11h{h} A: \" + \" \".join(f\"{t[j]!r}={ra[j]:.2f}\" for j in np.argsort(-ra)[:4]) + \" | B: \" + \" \".join(f\"{t[j]!r}={rb[j]:.2f}\" for j in np.argsort(-rb)[:4]))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11381,"cache_read_input_tokens":106149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11381},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29320cac-ee3e-4830-b982-1771b47076f9","uuid":"3798a69b-5882-4671-aa79-8fe18bca8420","timestamp":"2026-11-07T08:43:33.682Z","request_id":"req_011CgoGiCpZa8JRhctfYNmPq"}
